data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR is a genetic engineering technique that can modify the genomes of living organisms. It is based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [6].\n- Cas9 with a synthetic guide RNA is delivered into a cell and cuts the genome at a desired location, allowing genes to be removed or new ones added [6].\n- Cas9 acts as \"genetic scissors.\" The cell may repair the cut with errors that break or turn off the gene, or researchers can insert a new DNA template to add a new function [3].\n- Scientists commonly use CRISPR to knock out genes. They can also supply a repair template to replace a disease-causing mutation with a healthy sequence [2].\n- Headlines only: a Hacker News item links to an NPR story on CRISPR opening the door to treating diseases [4], and another reports HIV eliminated in cell culture using CRISPR-Cas editing [5]."}

